# Background › Miscellaneous

* [`crystals.crystal`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Background-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_crystals___crystal_FLQQ_--statement) — Crystal. Kind: Errors in the text. Category: Error or typo (for the editors to decide).

  As written a crystal is any nonempty set with arbitrary maps wt, eᵢ and fᵢ, with no axiom relating them, so the definition is degenerate. Kashiwara's definition adds maps εᵢ, φᵢ : B → ℤ ⊔ \{−∞\} with φᵢ(b) = εᵢ(b) + ⟨hᵢ, wt(b)⟩, and requires, among other axioms, that fᵢ(b) = b′ if and only if eᵢ(b′) = b, in which case wt(b′) = wt(b) − αᵢ, εᵢ(b′) = εᵢ(b) + 1 and φᵢ(b′) = φᵢ(b) − 1. The editors should state these axioms, or those of the narrower seminormal crystals, in which εᵢ(b) and φᵢ(b) are the largest k with eᵢᵏ(b) ≠ 0, respectively fᵢᵏ(b) ≠ 0.

* [`crystals.highest_weight`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Background-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_crystals___highest_weight_FLQQ_--statement) — Highest weight crystals. Kind: Errors in the text. Category: Error or typo (for the editors to decide).

  With only "has a highest weight element", the knowl's labelling by the weight of the highest weight element is ill-defined (e.g. for B(λ) ⊔ B(μ), which has two highest weight elements of different weights); the standard notion requires the crystal to be generated by its highest weight element (the round of 31 October review).

  Old:

  ```
  Highest weight crystals are {{ KNOWL('crystals.crystal') }} which have a highest weight element.
  ```

  New:

  ```
  Highest weight crystals are {{ KNOWL('crystals.crystal') }} generated by a highest weight element.
  ```

* [`crystals.littelmann_path`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Background-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_crystals___littelmann_path_FLQQ_--statement) — Littelmann path model for crystals. Kind: Errors in the text. Category: Error or typo.

  Garbled: π(0) = 0 is a condition, and what is required of the endpoint is that π(1) is a weight, an element of P (the path takes values in P ⊗ ℚ).

  Old:

  ```
  such that $\pi(0) = 0$ and $\pi(1)$ are weights.
  ```

  New:

  ```
  such that $\pi(0) = 0$ and $\pi(1)$ is a weight (an element of $P$).
  ```

