  The multiplier should be r(gamma), not r(g); T\_1(p) is not defined (the generator is T\_1(p\^2)); det\^\{otimes\} is missing its exponent k. Also: The paramodular groups (group.paramodular, paramodulargroup.sp2gr) are discrete subgroups of Sp(2g, ℚ) with non-integral entries, and for them L(Γ, S) is not a Γ-module. For g = 2 and N ≥ 2, γ = (1₂, diag(0, 1/N); 0, 1₂) lies in K(N) and α = diag(P, P) with P = (0, 1; 1, 0) lies in Sp(4, ℤ) ⊆ S, but no element of K(N)αγ is integral: K(N) preserves the lattice ℤ ⊕ (1/N)ℤ ⊕ ℤ ⊕ ℤ (the symplectic dual of ℤ ⊕ ℤ ⊕ ℤ ⊕ Nℤ), which contains ℤ⁴ but not the fourth column (1/N, 0, 1, 0) of αγ. So K(N)αγ is not K(N)β for any β ∈ S. The semigroup S\_Γ generated by Γ and S is the smallest repair, and is S when Γ ⊆ Sp(2g, ℤ); S\_p must then be taken inside S\_Γ. Also, identifying a double coset with the sum of its single cosets needs finitely many of them, so Γ must be arithmetic, not merely discrete: for Γ generated by (1, 1; 0, 1) in Sp(2, ℚ) = SL(2, ℚ) and α = (1, 0; 1, 1), Γ ∩ α⁻¹Γα is trivial and ΓαΓ is an infinite union of right cosets. Also: The multiplier should be r(gamma), not r(g); T\_1(p) is not defined (the generator is T\_1(p\^2)); det\^\{otimes\} is missing its exponent k. Both generation statements omit the scalar T\_0(p\^2) = Gamma (p 1\_\{2g\}) Gamma, so both are false (round ZW, superseding the earlier pair T\_1(p) -\> T\_1(p\^2)): the local Hecke ring is graded by the power of p in the multiplier, its degree-2 part has the basis T\_0(p\^2), ..., T\_g(p\^2) of g + 1 elements, and the proposed generators span at most g of these dimensions. For g = 2 the corrected statement is proved in LeanBridge (mem\_of\_heckeGenerators). Also: For B(N) ⊆ Γ ⊊ Sp(2g, ℤ), for example Γ = B(N) with N ≥ 2, the generation statement is false even with T₀(p²) added: S\_p contains Sp(2g, ℤ) (multiplier p⁰ = 1), so 𝓗\_p(Γ) contains the double cosets ΓσΓ with σ ∈ Sp(2g, ℤ) ∖ Γ, while T(p) and the T\_i(p²) have multipliers p and p², so that, the multiplier being multiplicative, the algebra they generate has its multiplier-1 part spanned by the identity Γ. The classical theorem (Andrianov) is for Γ = Sp(2g, ℤ), as proposed; to keep a level N the editors would need p ∤ N and a Hecke semigroup restricted modulo N, as in Andrianov's Hecke rings of Γ₀(N). Also, the link mf.borel\_subgroup points to no knowl.

